          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Post-War Challenges and Health Issues</strong><br>\nAfter World War I, Wilson faced challenges with the Treaty of Versailles and the League of Nations treaty, leading to a political struggle that he lost when the Senate rejected the League's charter.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Death</strong><br>\nWilson's later years in office were marked by a severe stroke that left him partially paralyzed. After his presidency, he lived in Washington, D.C., until his death on February 3, 1924.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Historical View</strong><br>\nWoodrow Wilson is often ranked highly for his leadership during World War I and for his advocacy for democracy and international peace. Despite this, his legacy is also critically viewed concerning his policies on race relations and civil liberties.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
